  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 1.* $v = f lambda = 20 times 15 = 300$ m s⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 2.* Sound is a mechanical wave and needs a medium; the Moon has no atmosphere.
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 3.* The rays only appear to come from behind the mirror, and left and right are swapped.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 4.* $sin r = sin 30°\/1.5 = 0.333$, so $r = 19°$ — the ray bends towards the normal.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 5.* $1\/v = 1\/10 - 1\/15 = 1\/30$, so $v = 30$ cm, real and inverted.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Violet has the largest refractive index, so it is slowed and deviated most.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 1.* The fundamental has $lambda = 2L = 1.0$ m, so $v = 200 times 1.0 = 200$ m s⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 2.* $v = 2d\/t = (2 times 204)\/1.2 = 340$ m s⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 3.* $1\/u = 1\/8 - 1\/24 = 2\/24$, so $u = 12$ cm.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 4.* $sin c = 1\/1.5 = 0.667$, so $c = 42°$.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 5.* $P = 1\/0.20 = 5.0$ dioptres.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Red, green and blue add to white; the pigment primaries are cyan, magenta and yellow.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 1.* $v = f lambda = 500 times 0.20 = 100$ m s⁻¹.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 2.* The beat frequency is the difference: $444 - 440 = 4$ per second.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 3.* A convex mirror diverges the light, so the image is always virtual, upright and diminished.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 4.* Apparent depth $= 4.0\/1.33 = 3.0$ m.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 5.* $M = f_0\/f_e = 120\/5.0 = 24$.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Every member of the spectrum travels at $3.0 times 10^8$ m s⁻¹ in a vacuum.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 1.* The pattern repeats every half wavelength, so neighbouring nodes are $lambda\/2$ apart.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 2.* Pitch is the ear's reading of frequency; amplitude gives loudness.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 3.* The angle of incidence is $90° - 25° = 65°$ from the normal, and the reflected angle equals it.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 4.* The frequency is set by the source; the speed, wavelength and direction all change.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 5.* Short sight needs a diverging lens to push the image back onto the retina.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 6.* The object reflects only red, and green light contains none, so it looks black.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 1.* The harmonics of a string are whole multiples: $3 times 120 = 360$ Hz.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 2.* A closed pipe sounds at odd harmonics: $3 times 170 = 510$ Hz.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 3.* Rays from the focus leave parallel to the axis and never meet, so the image is at infinity.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 4.* 50° is greater than the critical angle of 42°, so the light is totally internally reflected.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 5.* Powers in contact add: $4.0 + (-1.0) = +3.0$ D.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 6.* $E = I\/d^2 = 40\/4.0 = 10$ lux.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 1.* The frequency is fixed by the source; the speed falls in shallow water and the wavelength falls with it.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 2.* $d = v t \/ 2 = 340 times 0.50 \/ 2 = 85$ m.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 3.* $f = r\/2 = 40\/2 = 20$ cm.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 4.* $n = sin i \/ sin r$, with both angles measured from the normal.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 5.* $P = 1\/(-0.30) = -3.3$ D: negative, because the lens diverges.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 6.* Radio waves have the longest wavelengths and the lowest frequencies in the spectrum.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 1.* Two identical waves travelling in opposite directions superpose to give a stationary wave.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 2.* The wavefronts ahead are crowded together, so more crests reach the observer each second.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
    *Chapter 3.* With the face inside the focus a concave mirror gives an upright, magnified virtual image.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: fit-pad("mock1"))[
